  "notes": "The acid-jazz-funk feeder into neo-soul, windowed 1990–1997 per Gate 2 (the 1985 formation era is excluded by the entry itself). The research calls this \"the fastest habitual band in this section, because the idiom is 70s dance-funk rather than slow soul\", which is why the band sits at 96–116 — above every other artist in this batch — and why the whole kit is louder and busier: \"live funk kit, tight and dry — kick on 1 and the 'and' of 2, snare hard on 2&4 with ghost notes on the surrounding 16ths, continuous 16th hats with the pedal opening on the offbeat, and cowbell/conga overlay\". So `secondaryAnchor` is authored as 2&, the ghost probability fills six 16th positions, the hat is continuous at a fill density of 0.7, and `openHat.prob` 0.55 across all four offbeats is the pedal-opening rule stated literally. Harmony is riff-derived rather than cadential — \"dorian vamps on m7 and m9 chords, 2-bar cycles\" — so no families are authored, `maxChords` drops to 3 and the harmonic rhythm is vamp-weighted. The stated hook is the bass: \"slapped or fingered 16ths, syncopated against the kick and mixed as loud as the vocal; the bass line *is* the hook\", hence the lowest root probability and the highest anticipation here, with the register up to 50 and the chord voicing lifted to 52. The horn section \"answers every vocal phrase\", which is the counter density of 0.7. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are all drum probabilities and velocities and every melody number; the research marks tempo and drum detail as funk-idiom inference and [LOW-CONF]. ⚠ The id takes the heading up to the slash.",
